{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.HeckeJucysMurphyAdjacentNormalForm","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.HeckeJucysMurphyAdjacentNormalForm","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.HeckeJucysMurphyReduction"],"declarations":7,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.jmRaw_adjacent_of_comm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.jmRaw_adjacent_of_comm","kind":"theorem","line":163,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeJucysMurphyAdjacentNormalForm.lean#L163-L208","doc":"**Sufficient condition: an `N` commuting with both `u` and `v` satisfies the identity.**\n\nIf `N` commutes with `σ_k` and `σ_{k+1}` it commutes with everything in sight, so both normal forms\ncollapse to `N² · (uv)³` and `N² · (uvu)(uv)u` respectively, and `braid_cube_eq` finishes.\n\n**This is sufficient, NOT necessary** — and the distinction is measured, not guessed.  At `k = 2` in\n`H₅`, `N = σ₃² = v²` satisfies the identity while *not* commuting with `u = σ₂` (they are adjacent).\nSo this condition explains part of the data and cannot be the characterisation.\n\nIt also does **not** apply to the case that matters: `N = L_k` does not commute with `σ_k` — the\nhook word `L_k` ends in `σ_{k-1}`, adjacent to `σ_k` — which is exactly why adjacent commutativity\nis hard and why this lemma does not close it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.braid_cube_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.braid_cube_eq","kind":"theorem","line":149,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeJucysMurphyAdjacentNormalForm.lean#L149-L161","doc":"**`(uv)³ = (uvu)(uv)u`** at every index — both sides are `(uvu)²`.\n\nThe left side regroups as `(uvu)(vuv)` and one braid move turns the second factor into `uvu`; the\nright side is `(uvu)(uvu)` by associativity alone.  This is the index-`k` form of the half-twist\nidentity `fullTwist3_symm`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.jmRaw_adjacent_iff_normal_forms","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.jmRaw_adjacent_iff_normal_forms","kind":"theorem","line":130,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeJucysMurphyAdjacentNormalForm.lean#L130-L139","doc":"**The open question, stated once.**  Adjacent commutativity at `k+1` holds exactly when the two\nnormal forms agree.  This is a restatement, not a proof — but it isolates the problem as a single\nword identity in the one unknown `N = L_k`, with no recursion left to unfold. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.jmRaw_adjacent_right_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.jmRaw_adjacent_right_zero","kind":"theorem","line":122,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeJucysMurphyAdjacentNormalForm.lean#L122-L128","doc":"At `k = 0` the right form collapses to `(σ₀σ₁σ₀)(σ₀σ₁)σ₀`, which `fullTwist3_symm` identifies\nwith the left one — recovering `jmRaw_comm_adjacent_one` as the `N = 1` specialisation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.jmRaw_adjacent_left_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.jmRaw_adjacent_left_zero","kind":"theorem","line":116,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeJucysMurphyAdjacentNormalForm.lean#L116-L120","doc":"At `k = 0` the left form collapses to `(σ₀σ₁)³` — matching `jmRaw_prod_three`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.jmRaw_adjacent_right","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.jmRaw_adjacent_right","kind":"theorem","line":89,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeJucysMurphyAdjacentNormalForm.lean#L89-L112","doc":"**Right normal form**: `L_{k+2} L_{k+1} = (uvu) N (uv) N u`.  Same two rewrites, applied to the\nmirrored word — note it ends in `u`, not `uv`, which is the whole difference from the left form. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.jmRaw_adjacent_left","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.Hecke.jmRaw_adjacent_left","kind":"theorem","line":66,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/HeckeJucysMurphyAdjacentNormalForm.lean#L66-L87","doc":"**Left normal form**: `L_{k+1} L_{k+2} = (uv) N (uv) N (uv)` with `u = σ_k`, `v = σ_{k+1}`,\n`N = L_k`.  One braid move, one commutation of `N` past `v`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}